 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "31431bb9",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# 10 – Models: does a model trained before the 2018 change still work after it?\n",
    "**Input:** `model_data.csv` (from `09_Model_Data.ipynb`)\n",
    "\n",
    "We train three classification models from the course – **Logistic Regression** (Chapter 12), **Decision Tree** (Chapter 9) and **K-Nearest Neighbors** (Chapter 12) – and test them in three experiments:\n",
    "\n",
    "| Experiment | Train on | Test on |\n",
    "| --- | --- | --- |\n",
    "| **H2** (main question) | 80% of Pre | the other 20% of Pre **and** Post |\n",
    "| **H3** (placebo) | 80% of Pre before 1 Jan 2017 | the other 20% of it **and** Pre after 1 Jan 2017 |\n",
    "| **Reference** (random split) | random 80% of Pre + Post | the other 20% |\n",
    "\n",
    "Because only about 1 of 5 inspections fails, we measure **accuracy, sensitivity, specificity, PPV** and the **AUC** (area under the ROC curve), not only accuracy (Chapter 12). Because the data is imbalanced, a model rarely gives a probability above 0.5, so with the usual cutoff of 0.5 it would almost never say \"Fail\". We therefore use the **fail rate of the training data as the cutoff** (Section 1)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 1,
   "id": "f70ce77e",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split, KFold\n",
    "from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\n",
    "from sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\n",
    "from sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeClassifier, plot_tree\n",
    "from sklearn.neighbors import KNeighborsClassifier\n",
    "from sklearn.metrics import confusion_matrix, roc_auc_score, roc_curve\n",
    "import warnings\n",
    "warnings.filterwarnings('ignore')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "3ccb63ad",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "pandas, numpy and matplotlib as always; from scikit-learn the train/test split and KFold (Chapter 10), StandardScaler for the scaling, the three models, and the metrics: the confusion matrix (TP, FP, TN, FN), the ROC curve and its AUC (Chapter 12)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 2,
   "id": "e23a72a7",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "(126787, 88)\n",
      "Number of features: 83\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "data = pd.read_csv('model_data.csv')\n",
    "id_columns = ['Inspection ID', 'Inspection Date', 'Period', 'Placebo_Period', 'Fail']\n",
    "features = [c for c in data.columns if c not in id_columns]\n",
    "print(data.shape)\n",
    "print('Number of features:', len(features))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "0990af70",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "126787 rows and 88 columns, 83 of the columns are features – 12 numeric and 71 dummies."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "35da95fa",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 1. Helper functions"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 3,
   "id": "600d5b25",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def evaluate(y_true, prob, cutoff=0.5):\n",
    "    # prob = predicted probability of Fail; above the cutoff we predict Fail\n",
    "    y_pred = (prob >= cutoff).astype(int)\n",
    "    tn, fp, fn, tp = confusion_matrix(y_true, y_pred, labels=[0, 1]).ravel()\n",
    "    return {'Accuracy': (tp + tn) / (tp + tn + fp + fn),\n",
    "            'Sensitivity': tp / (tp + fn),\n",
    "            'Specificity': tn / (tn + fp),\n",
    "            'PPV': tp / (tp + fp) if (tp + fp) > 0 else 0,\n",
    "            'AUC': roc_auc_score(y_true, prob)}"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "6c6984bc",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "`evaluate` calculates the metrics of Chapter 12 from the confusion matrix (Positive = **Fail**):\n",
    "- **Accuracy** = (TP + TN) / all\n",
    "- **Sensitivity** = TP / (TP + FN): of the inspections that really failed, how many did the model find? (the most important one for us: a missed failing restaurant is the dangerous mistake)\n",
    "- **Specificity** = TN / (TN + FP): of the inspections that passed, how many did the model call \"Pass\"?\n",
    "- **PPV** = TP / (TP + FP): when the model says \"Fail\", how often is it right?\n",
    "- **AUC**: area under the ROC curve, one number for all cutoffs (0.5 = random guessing, 1 = perfect)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 4,
   "id": "7225db59",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def make_models(knn_k, tree_depth):\n",
    "    return {'Logistic Regression': LogisticRegression(max_iter=1000),\n",
    "            'Decision Tree': DecisionTreeClassifier(max_depth=tree_depth, random_state=42),\n",
    "            'KNN': KNeighborsClassifier(n_neighbors=knn_k)}\n",
    "\n",
    "def run_experiment(name, train, tests, knn_k, tree_depth):\n",
    "    # scaling: fitted on the training data only, then applied to every test set (no leakage)\n",
    "    scaler = StandardScaler()\n",
    "    X_train = scaler.fit_transform(train[features])\n",
    "    y_train = train['Fail']\n",
    "    cutoff = y_train.mean()   # fail rate of the training data (about 0.21)\n",
    "    rows = []\n",
    "    for model_name, model in make_models(knn_k, tree_depth).items():\n",
    "        model.fit(X_train, y_train)\n",
    "        for test_name, test in tests.items():\n",
    "            prob = model.predict_proba(scaler.transform(test[features]))[:, 1]\n",
    "            result = evaluate(test['Fail'], prob, cutoff)\n",
    "            result.update({'Experiment': name, 'Model': model_name, 'Tested on': test_name, 'Cutoff': round(cutoff, 3)})\n",
    "            rows.append(result)\n",
    "    return pd.DataFrame(rows)[['Experiment', 'Model', 'Tested on', 'Cutoff', 'Accuracy', 'Sensitivity', 'Specificity', 'PPV', 'AUC']]"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b21863c4",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "`make_models` creates the three models. `run_experiment` scales the features with **StandardScaler** (mean 0, std 1), fitted on the **training data only** and then used on the test sets, so no information from the test data leaks into training. Scaling is needed for KNN, which uses distances (otherwise Days_Since_First_Seen, in thousands, would dominate Prev_Fail, 0 or 1 – Chapter 11); logistic regression also trains better with scaled features, and the decision tree is not affected. Then every model is trained once and tested on every test set.\n",
    "\n",
    "**The cutoff (Chapter 12, \"changing the cutoff\").** Only about 1 of 5 inspections fails, so the models rarely give a probability of Fail above 0.5; with the usual cutoff of 0.5 they would call almost everything \"Pass\" (sensitivity close to 0). Instead, `run_experiment` uses the **fail rate of the training data** as the cutoff (about 0.21): an inspection is predicted as \"Fail\" when its probability of failing is **higher than the average**. The cutoff comes only from the training data, so no test information is used, and in each experiment it is the same for the test on the own period and on the later period, so the two results can be compared. Section 9 shows how the results change with other cutoffs."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ed5b2bf9",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 2. Split the data"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 5,
   "id": "92c048dd",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Pre train            51434 rows   fail rate 0.216\n",
      "Pre test             12859 rows   fail rate 0.216\n",
