  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "86779e0a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "All models use the cutoff 0.216 (the fail rate of the Pre training data). **AUC:** logistic regression is the best model on Pre (0.690), then the decision tree (0.674) and KNN (0.664); on Post all three are lower (0.643, 0.647, 0.607). **At the cutoff**, the models find most failing inspections: sensitivity 0.712 (logistic regression), 0.785 (tree) and 0.624 (KNN) on Pre, and almost the same on Post (0.700, 0.810, 0.597). What gets worse after the change is mainly the **specificity** (0.562 → 0.503, 0.471 → 0.389, 0.610 → 0.559): on Post the models give a high probability of failing to more inspections that then pass, so they create more false alarms. The PPV stays about 0.29–0.31 everywhere: when a model says \"Fail\", it is right about 3 times out of 10, which is still better than picking at random (22–24%).\n",
    "\n",
    "Note: the accuracy (0.49–0.61) is now **lower** than the 78% of a model that always says \"Pass\". This is expected with a low cutoff: we accept more false alarms to find about 70% of the failing restaurants instead of 0% (Chapter 12, \"Is accuracy enough?\")."
   ]
  },
