    "def drop_table(results, same, later):\n",
    "    a = results[results['Tested on'] == same].set_index('Model')\n",
    "    b = results[results['Tested on'] == later].set_index('Model')\n",
    "    return (b[['Accuracy', 'Sensitivity', 'Specificity', 'PPV', 'AUC']] - a[['Accuracy', 'Sensitivity', 'Specificity', 'PPV', 'AUC']])\n",
    "\n",
    "drops = pd.concat({'2018 change (H2)': drop_table(h2, 'Pre (same period)', 'Post (after change)'),\n",
    "                   '2017 placebo (H3)': drop_table(h3, 'Before 2017 (same period)', 'After 2017 (placebo)')})\n",
    "drops.round(3)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "a87cc69e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Change of every metric when the model moves from its own period to the later period (negative = worse). We compare the **AUC**, because it does not depend on the cutoff:\n",
    "\n",
    "| Model | Drop at the 2018 change (H2) | Drop at the 2017 placebo (H3) |\n",
    "| --- | --- | --- |\n",
    "| Logistic regression | −0.048 | **−0.057** |\n",
    "| Decision tree | **−0.027** | −0.010 |\n",
    "| KNN | −0.057 | **−0.073** |\n",
    "\n",
    "The models lose performance after 2018 (**H2 supported**), but at the placebo date, where the law did **not** change, the drop is just as large or larger for logistic regression and KNN. Only the decision tree drops more at 2018, and the difference is small (0.017). So the drop after 2018 is **not clearly larger** than the normal change over time (**H3 not supported**): the loss of performance is mainly **ordinary temporal shift**, and we cannot show that the new food code itself made the models worse. At the cutoff, the type of error is different (after 2018 mainly the specificity falls, after 2017 mainly the sensitivity), but the size of the change is similar."
   ]
  },
