  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "79494de6",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "With the usual cutoff 0.5 the model says \"Fail\" only when it is quite sure; because only about 22% of the inspections fail, it then misses many failing restaurants (low sensitivity). A lower cutoff finds more of them (higher sensitivity) but also sends inspectors to more clean places (lower specificity) – the trade-off of Chapter 12. Because a missed failing restaurant is the dangerous mistake, a lower cutoff can make sense. The cutoff we use in the experiments (the training fail rate, about 0.21) lies between 0.2 and 0.3, where sensitivity and specificity are both reasonable. At **0.2** the model finds 76.4% (Pre) and 74.8% (Post) of the failing inspections with a specificity of 0.504 and 0.453; at **0.3** it finds only 38.9% and 44.0%, with a specificity of 0.817 and 0.733. Between 0.2 and 0.3 the sensitivity falls quickly, because many inspections get a probability in this range. At 0.5 the model finds almost nothing (2.1% and 7.4%), which is why we do not use the default cutoff. The Pre → Post change is not the same at every cutoff: at low cutoffs the specificity falls, at 0.3–0.5 the sensitivity even rises a little on Post, because the Post inspections get higher probabilities in general."
   ]
  },
